                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-6">The simulator reads live rules from <code>/api/rt/config</code> on load. When that call succeeds, the badge reads <strong>Live rules</strong> in green. When it fails, the fallback values ship and the badge reads <strong>Default rules</strong> in amber. Either way the rules shown match what the live worker enforces.</p>
                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left"><a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a></p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- SECTION 12 -->
            <section id="equal" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">12. Equal authority — no admins</h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">There is no <code>admin</code> table in the database. There is no <code>is_admin</code> flag on any user row. There is no endpoint that any user can call to override, delete, or rewrite a governance decision, no matter who they are.</p>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-4">What exists instead:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Every action is a proposal.</strong> Changing a threshold, adding a rule, updating the constitution — all of it is a Meta proposal. None of it can happen outside the vote.</li>
                  <li><strong>Every action is on the chain.</strong> Deleting a row would break the audit chain and immediately be visible. The only way to reverse a decision is the reconsider window.</li>
                  <li><strong>The maintainer can only merge code.</strong> The maintainer-powers document specifies exactly what the person running the site can do: merge a PR after a Code proposal passes, apply a Config change the system already queued, and run the R2 attachment purge. Nothing else.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-6">This is unusual, and it's intentional. Most "decentralised" systems quietly keep an admin backdoor. This one doesn't. If the community wants to change a rule, it needs the community to agree to the change. If it wants to remove the maintainer, it can — by voting a new one in.</p>
                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left"><a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a></p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- SECTION 13 -->
            <section id="amend" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">13. Amending the constitution</h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">The constitution is self-amending. It can be changed — but only by the same process it describes, and only by clearing the highest barrier on the site. A Meta proposal to change the constitution requires:</p>
                <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>75% YES</strong> — higher than any other kind.</li>
                  <li><strong>Under 25% NO</strong> — the block threshold still applies.</li>
                  <li><strong>Quorum met</strong> — at least max(10, 20% of eligible).</li>
                  <li><strong>72-hour apply delay</strong> — so the community has three full days to reconsider before the change lands.</li>
                  <li><strong>30-day reconsider window</strong> — 10% can trigger a second vote at any point in that window.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-6">The current constitution is published at <a href="/community/round-table/constitution/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/community/round-table/constitution/</a>. The founding document is at <a href="/community/round-table/founding/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/community/round-table/founding/</a>. Every amendment is recorded on the audit chain.</p>
                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left"><a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a></p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- FAQ -->
            <section id="faq" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black mt-16 mb-12 text-center bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-400 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">14. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
              <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-4">

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">What is the Round Table?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Traffic Torch's community governance system. Every eligible user has one vote, no administrators exist inside the system, and every decision is hash-chained and publically verifiable. Proposals move through Propose → Discuss → Vote → Execute with fixed rules that can only be changed by another vote.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Do I need an account to view the Round Table?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>No. All proposals, votes, comments, bench activity, and the audit chain are publicly readable. You only need an account to submit a proposal, cast a vote, or leave a comment.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">How is eligibility determined?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>An account must be at least 7 days old AND have either a verified profile (email verification or higher) OR a public profile. This is the exact rule the server enforces. It prevents brand-new accounts from voting while remaining open to anyone who has actually participated.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">What are the four kinds of proposal?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Advisory records community signal but applies no automatic change. Config changes a config key and applies immediately on pass. Meta changes the rules, thresholds, or the constitution; it needs 75% YES and applies after a 72-hour delay. Code links to a pull request that a human must merge — nothing auto-merges.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Why is the meta threshold higher?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Meta proposals change the rules that govern every other proposal. A 75% threshold instead of 66% adds friction precisely where the risk of gaming is highest. Combined with the 72-hour apply delay and the 30-day reconsider window, a rule change can only stick if it survives multiple rounds of scrutiny.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">What is the 25% block threshold for?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>The block protects minority positions. If 25% or more of votes cast are NO, the proposal fails regardless of how many YES votes it received. Without this, a bare 66% majority could steamroll a substantial opposition on every decision. The block makes 66% necessary but not sufficient.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Can a bare majority pass anything?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>No. Config, Code, and Advisory proposals need 66% YES AND under 25% NO. Meta proposals need 75% YES AND under 25% NO. Quorum must also be met. A proposal that clears the pass threshold can still fail on the block.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Can a NO minority block a proposal even at 90% YES?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Yes. If NO reaches 25% of votes cast, the proposal fails even at 90% YES. This is intentional: the block exists to stop bare majorities from overriding substantial opposition. The only way to reverse a passed proposal is the reconsider window, and the only way to reverse a blocked proposal is a new proposal with lower opposition.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">How long do votes last?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Every vote runs 5 days from open to close, regardless of proposal kind. Comment periods differ: Meta 30 days, Config 14 days, Code 14 days, Advisory 7 days. An Objector's Bench pause extends the vote deadline by 72 hours without reducing voters' time.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">What happens if quorum isn't met?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>The proposal returns outcome <code>no_quorum</code> and fails. Quorum is the larger of 10 votes or 20% of eligible. It prevents a tiny minority from closing a proposal when the electorate has not engaged. A no_quorum failure does not consume the proposer's 14-day cooldown as harshly as a rejection.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">How do I submit a proposal?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Log in, go to <a href="/community/round-table/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/community/round-table/</a>, click New Proposal, choose a kind and category, write the body in Markdown, and submit. The proposal enters Discuss immediately and cannot open a vote until the comment period for its kind has elapsed.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">What is the Objector's Bench?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>A small randomly-drawn group of eligible users who can pause any open vote for 72 hours by filing a public objection. It can only slow a vote — it cannot pass, block, or alter any proposal. Each member can file one objection per proposal with a 30-day cooldown, and a pause extends the vote deadline by 72 hours so voters keep their full 5 days.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Can the Bench block or pass a proposal?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>No. The Bench can only pause. It has no veto, no approval, and no way to alter the outcome. A bench pause is an attention device — it forces extra discussion by extending the vote by 72 hours. The actual decision is still made by the full electorate.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">How do I trigger a reconsider?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Within 30 days of a proposal passing, 10% of eligible users can sign a reconsider request. If that threshold is reached, a new vote opens on the same proposal with the same rules. A second-pass rejection reverses the original resolution; a second-pass approval confirms it.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">How do I verify a vote result?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Open the proposal's page and look for the audit chain entries. Every vote, comment, close, and execute event is hashed with SHA-256 and chained to the previous entry. <code>/api/rt/audit/verify</code> returns an intact-or-broken status for any range. The simulator's audit ticker demonstrates this live — click Tamper on any block and every downstream block turns red.</p></div></details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden"><summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 transition">Who can change the constitution?<span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></summary><div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p>Only a Meta proposal. The constitution is self-amending: it can be changed by the same voting mechanism it defines, and only by clearing 75% YES with under 25% NO, then surviving the 72-hour apply delay and the 30-day reconsider window. No administrator, no founder privilege, no override.</p></div></details>

              </div>
              <p class="mt-6 text-center md:text-left text-sm"><a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a></p>
            </section>

